CORE broken-smt-backend
compound-assignment-operand-width2.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always \(##1 main\.a == 0\): PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Compound assignment "a >>= b" with a wide shift amount (8-bit a, 16-bit b ==
256). Per IEEE 1800-2017 11.4.1, "a >>= b" is "a = a >> (b)", and the right
operand of a shift is self-determined (11.6.1/11.8.2), so the shift amount
keeps its 16-bit width: 0xff >> 256 == 0, so a must become 0.

EBMC wrongly REFUTES the property: it truncates b to the 8-bit type of a
before the shift (256 % 256 == 0, i.e. a shift by 0), so a stays 0xff. The
root cause spans two files: verilog_typecheckt::convert_assign in
src/verilog/verilog_typecheck.cpp treats every verilog_blocking_assign_*
compound form like a plain assignment and calls assignment_conversion(rhs,
lhs.type()), truncating the (self-determined) shift amount to lhs's type;
and verilog_rtl_buildert::build_compound_assign in
src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp then builds the operation with the
already-truncated operand (binary_exprt{lhs, op, rhs, rhs.type()}). Found
while auditing src/verilog/verilog_rtl.cpp.

Note: tagged broken-smt-backend. With the front-end fix the minisat/bit-level
engine correctly proves this, but the SMT2 bitvector back-end evaluates the
shift distance modulo the (narrower) left-operand width, so "a >> 256" is
treated as "a >> 0" and the property is spuriously REFUTED. That is a separate
back-end shift-width issue and is excluded from the --z3/--cvc5 runs.
